body:
## Summary

On Node 20.20.2 and 22.23.1, cancelling `systemOne` after response headers arrive can terminate the process with an unhandled native `AbortError`, even after the caller successfully catches the SDK's `APIUserAbortError`.

This is a **dependency-origin compatibility report**, not a newly discovered Undici defect. The SDK's cloned-response buffering path encounters behavior already addressed upstream in [Undici #4804](https://github.com/nodejs/undici/pull/4804). The same test passes on Node 24.21.0.

## Affected version and environment

- `@typesafe-ai/sdk@0.6.0` from npm.
- Also reproduced on a clean build of `main` at `66880ccded6cb642dc1809620c2b108c33730214` (`v0.6.0`). Local and published tarballs were byte-identical.
- Linux x86_64 / WSL2.
- Node 20.20.2 / bundled Undici 6.24.1: fails.
- Node 22.23.1 / bundled Undici 6.27.0: fails.
- Node 24.21.0 / bundled Undici 7.29.1: passes.
- Both ESM and CommonJS bundles were exercised in independent package consumers.

## Supported workflow and expected behavior

Cancel a `systemOne` call through `RequestOptions.signal` while the response body is still arriving, and handle its rejection. The documented `APIUserAbortError` should be catchable without a separate unhandled rejection terminating the application.

Version-pinned contract:

- [README: Node 20 or newer](https://github.com/typesafe-ai/typesafe-sdk-js/blob/66880ccded6cb642dc1809620c2b108c33730214/README.md#L7-L10).
- [Public cancellation option](https://github.com/typesafe-ai/typesafe-sdk-js/blob/66880ccded6cb642dc1809620c2b108c33730214/src/types.ts#L196-L205).
- [systemOne documented cancellation error](https://github.com/typesafe-ai/typesafe-sdk-js/blob/66880ccded6cb642dc1809620c2b108c33730214/src/client.ts#L291-L300).

## Minimal reproduction

In a fresh consumer directory using Node 22.23.1:

```sh
npm init -y
npm install --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund @typesafe-ai/sdk@0.6.0
```

Save the following as `reproduce.mjs`. It uses only a synthetic key and a loopback HTTP fixture, and makes no request to the TypeSafe API. `setImmediate` supplies an event-loop barrier after headers, rather than a timing-based sleep.

```js
// Run in a separate process: unhandled runtime rejections must not be swallowed by the runner.
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
import { createServer } from "node:http";
import { TypeSafeClient, APIUserAbortError, noul } from "@typesafe-ai/sdk";
const abort = process.argv[2] === "abort";
const controller = new AbortController();
const data = {
  model: "fixture",
  answers: { ok: { type: "noul", noul: 0.9 } },
  usage: { input_tokens: 1, output_tokens: 1 },
};
const server = createServer((_req, res) => {
  res.writeHead(200, { "content-type": "application/json" });
  if (abort) res.flushHeaders();
  else res.end(JSON.stringify(data));
});
await new Promise((resolve) => server.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", resolve));
try {
  const client = new TypeSafeClient({
    apiKey: "synthetic",
    baseURL: `http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}`,
    retry: { maxRetries: 0 },
    fetch: async (url, init) => {
      assert.equal(new URL(url).hostname, "127.0.0.1");
      const response = await fetch(url, init);
      // Event-loop barrier, not a timing-based sleep. SDK sees headers first.
      if (abort) setImmediate(() => controller.abort());
      return response;
    },
  });
  const pending = client.systemOne(
    { state: "synthetic", questions: { ok: noul("?") } },
    { signal: controller.signal },
  );
  if (abort) {
    await assert.rejects(pending, APIUserAbortError);
    console.log("PASS caught APIUserAbortError");
  } else {
    assert.deepEqual(await pending, data);
    console.log("PASS healthy response");
  }
  await new Promise((resolve) => setImmediate(resolve));
} finally {
  server.closeAllConnections();
  await new Promise((resolve) => server.close(resolve));
}
console.log("PASS normal process exit");
```

From that consumer directory:

```sh
node reproduce.mjs healthy
node reproduce.mjs abort
```

For Linux network isolation, the tested equivalent is:

```sh
unshare -Urn sh -c 'ip link set lo up && node reproduce.mjs healthy'
unshare -Urn sh -c 'ip link set lo up && node reproduce.mjs abort'
```

## Observed output

Healthy control on Node 22.23.1, exit 0:

```text
PASS healthy response
PASS normal process exit
```

Cancelled call on Node 22.23.1, exit 1:

```text
PASS caught APIUserAbortError
node:internal/process/promises:394
    triggerUncaughtException(err, true /* fromPromise */);
    ^

DOMException [AbortError]: This operation was aborted
```

The expected SDK error is already caught when the native rejection terminates the process. Node 20.20.2 has the same outcome. On Node 24.21.0, both modes exit 0; the abort mode prints both `PASS caught APIUserAbortError` and `PASS normal process exit`.

A child-process regression asserts that the healthy and handled-abort cases both exit 0. It gives **1 failed / 1 passed** on tested Node 20/22 and **2 passed** on tested Node 24. The Node 22 failure was repeated in a fresh process.

The unmodified repository suite already exposes related baseline evidence: on Node 22.23.1 it runs 189 passing assertions but exits 1 with eight unhandled abort errors; the same baseline suite passes on Node 24.21.0.

## Root cause and upstream status

The SDK [clones and drains response bodies](https://github.com/typesafe-ai/typesafe-sdk-js/blob/66880ccded6cb642dc1809620c2b108c33730214/src/client.ts#L180-L200), while [caller cancellation also aborts the native fetch](https://github.com/typesafe-ai/typesafe-sdk-js/blob/66880ccded6cb642dc1809620c2b108c33730214/src/client.ts#L407-L446).

The tested Node 22 bundled Undici `abortFetch` contains a `response.body.stream.cancel(error).catch(...)` handler that rethrows non-`ERR_INVALID_STATE` errors into a separate unobserved promise. Catching the SDK request does not handle that promise. A diagnostic using native fetch/clone/cancel without importing the SDK reproduces the failure, so ownership is not attributed solely to SDK code.

[Undici #4799](https://github.com/nodejs/undici/issues/4799) and merged [PR #4804](https://github.com/nodejs/undici/pull/4804) cover related cloned-response abort behavior. The [merged commit](https://github.com/nodejs/undici/commit/fc8bb75538276b597f80624ad5459aa63f813716) replaces the relevant cancel/rethrow path with stream erroring. Its patch was checked against the failing bundled runtime implementation.

## Confirmed impact and limitations

A normally handled cancellation can terminate a consumer process on the two tested supported Node 20/22 versions. Healthy requests work. This requires cancellation while the body remains in flight; it is not a claim that every request fails. Other Node patch releases, other runtimes, and the live API were not tested.

Suggested priority: **Medium, operational compatibility**, not a security severity. The tested Node 24 release is a working alternative. No separate novel upstream bug is claimed.

## Suggested fix direction

Choose a compatibility strategy for the advertised Node range: avoid the problematic clone/abort interaction while retaining raw-response semantics, use a verified fixed transport, or explicitly change/document the supported runtime requirement. Do not suppress process-wide unhandled rejections. Add a child-process regression to a supported-Node matrix so a correctly caught SDK error cannot hide a process-level failure.

No production fix was made or claimed verified.

## Duplicate check

Immediately before filing, refreshed current main (unchanged at the SHA above) and enumerated all public SDK issues and PRs, open and closed. The only existing item was the unrelated merged bootstrap publishing PR #1; there were no standalone issues or open PRs. Earlier all-state searches for `bufferResponse`, `AbortError`, and `APIUserAbortError` returned no matches; source history and release notes were also inspected.

The upstream Undici overlap is explicit above. This issue tracks SDK compatibility/user impact, rather than duplicating the upstream discovery.
